==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|wynsum||winsome, pleasant, joyful, merry|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Germanic|wunisamaz|joyful|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Germanic|title=|wunjō|joy, delight, desire|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|wun-||wenǝ-|to wish, love|lang=en}} + {{proto|Germanic|-samaz|-some|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|gmh|-}} {{term|wunnesam||winsome, joyful, delightful|lang=gmh}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|wynn||joy, rapture, pleasure, delight|lang=ang}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Wonne||bliss, delight, joy|lang=de}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈwɪn.sʌm/}} {{SAMPA|/"wIn.sVm/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[charming|Charming]]; inspiring [[trust]] and [[approval]]; especially if in an innocent manner.
#: ''His bedside manner was especially '''winsome'''.''

